tion (CDPD) algorithm, a novel method which is the cyclostationary counterpart of the DPD. ... This method is an extension of the conventional DPD method introduced by Weiss [18] to the case of cyclostationary signals. ... This research was supported by the Israel Science Foundation (grant No. 218/08) and by the Institute for Future Technologies Research named for the Medvedi, Shwartzman, and Gensler Families.\n\n  *   * ## Signal Processing\n\nDate: December 2009\n\nPages: 2448-2464\n\nVolume: Volume 89, Issue 12\n\n## Part of special issue\n\n# Direct position determination of cyclostationary signals☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Alit Mendelson Reuven, Anthony J. Weiss\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.sigpro.2009.04.009\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nDirect position determination (DPD) was recently proposed as a new method for passive localization of narrowband radio transmitters. Its superior performance, especially in the low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) regime, is ascribed to both the joint optimization it performs over all base stations that participate in the estimation process and the direct determination of the emitter position as opposed to conventional methods which typically process the information from the individual base stations separately and determine the location using two estimation steps.\n\nIn this paper, we introduce the cyclic direct position determination (CDPD) algorithm, a novel method which is the cyclostationary counterpart of the DPD. Despite its name, the CDPD is not a simple extension of the DPD but a unique algorithm which deviates substantially from its ancestor. Yet, the CDPD enjoys both the benefits of the DPD algorithm and the great performance robustness in the presence of narrowband interferences thanks to its cyclostationarity exploitation.\n\n## Keywords\n\nAngle of arrival (AOA)\n\n;\n\nArray processing\n\n;\n\nCyclostationarity\n\n;\n\nEmitter localization\n\n;\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe signal processing problem of passive source localization is of great interest since World War I. It has various applications in both civil and defense-oriented fields. Within the military applications we can mention localization systems for intelligence purposes, and electronic warfare while for civil applications we can count direction finding for navigation, vehicle location, and tracking systems, and law enforcement in general. In the decentralized approach the processing is divided into two stages. In the first stage, each base station (BS) performs an estimation of a specific parameter of the received signal such as impinging angle or propagation delay. Subsequently, in the second stage, these estimations are collected from all of the BSs to derive the location estimation. This mechanism was comprehensively discussed for both AOA and TDOA by Torrieri [17]. In the centralized approach, which the method of direct position determination (DPD) [18], [1] belongs to, the received samples from all of the BSs are conveyed to a common central unit which jointly processes the BSs data and estimates the transmitter location in a single step. Obviously, because of the incomplete data batch used by each BS processor and the over-parameterization, decentralized processing is sub-optimal unlike centralized processing as explained by Amar and Weiss [1].\n\nAnother distinction that can be made between different localization methods is related to their signal selectivity nature, meaning whether they are designed for the general case or for a specific signal type. Traditionally, most of the location finding algorithms which are based on AOA (e.g., MUSIC [15]) or TDOA (e.g., cross-correlation [3], [4]) were developed as general algorithms in the sense that they assume an arbitrary signal waveform and thus do not exploit any special property of the signal. However, almost every digital signal exhibits a unique property called (wide sense) cyclostationarity [8]. This property means that both the first and second moments of such a process are periodic in time as opposed to stationary processes. Naturally, due to the digital nature of communications nowadays a new class of localization algorithms which exploit the cyclostationarity property has evolved (e.g., [5], [11], [19]). This led to the development of many “cyclic” versions of existing conventional localization methods such as the cyclic MUSIC which was first introduced by Gardner [7] for the case of a single transmitter and afterwards was generalized to handle multiple emitters [14], and cyclic cross-correlation [9]. Obviously, the main advantage of the “cyclic” methods over their “conventional” counterparts is their ability to mitigate both thermal noise and interferences which do not share the same cycle frequency as the signals of interest (SOIs) and which can severely degrade the performance of the conventional methods.\n\nThe purpose of this paper is to present a new passive localization method for narrowband cyclostationary signals which combines the two concepts discussed above, namely, centralized processing and cyclostationarity exploitation. The algorithm, henceforth called cyclic direct position determination (CDPD), is the cyclic counterpart of the DPD algorithm which was first suggested by Weiss [18] for the case of a single transmitter, and afterwards was generalized to multiple transmitters [1]. However, as will be explained in detail later, the CDPD cannot be viewed as a mere expansion of the conventional DPD but as a unique algorithm with a novel cost function.\n\nThis paper is organized as follows: Section 2 introduces some basic properties of cyclostationary processes. Section 3 presents the model used throughout this paper. Section 4 includes the main part of this paper—derivation of the CDPD algorithm. Section 5 introduces a small error analysis of the CDPD algorithm in which a closed-form expression for the mean square error (MSE) of the CDPD is developed. Section 6 presents a numerical study of the CDPD performance using Monte Carlo simulations. This empirical performance is compared both to the MSE of other localization methods and to the analytical results developed in Section 4. Finally, Section 7 summaries this paper with concluding remarks.\n\n### Performance analysis\n\nThe goal of this section is to derive a closed-form expression for the MSE of the CDPD algorithm which will be later corroborated empirically using Monte Carlo simulations. Due to the high resemblance between the CADPD and cyclic MUSIC whose performance was already studied by some papers (e.g., [13]) we skip the MSE derivation of the CADPD algorithm and focus only on the performance analysis of the CTDPD estimator.\n\nIn order to facilitate the performance analysis of the CTDPD algorithm, we assume\n\n### Simulation results\n\nIn order to assess the performance of the devised algorithm and compare it with previous methods, we carried out an empirical study which simulates a typical cellular system. Such a system was already introduced in the context of the CTDPD ambiguity problem in Section 4.4. The Monte Carlo simulations were performed in two different kinds of noise environments, AWGN and a mixture of the latter with a cyclostationary interferer having a different cycle frequency than the SOI. The concrete setup\n\n### Conclusions\n\nIn this paper, we proposed a novel passive localization method for radio frequency signals called cyclic direct position determination. This method is an extension of the conventional DPD method introduced by Weiss [18] to the case of cyclostationary signals. While the conventional DPD does not make any assumptions on the SOI, the CDPD exploits the a priori information it has regarding the cycle frequency of the cyclostationary SOI in order to mitigate strong interferers. Thus, while the\n\n\nThis research was supported by the Israel Science Foundation (grant No. 218/08) and by the Institute for Future Technologies Research named for the Medvedi, Shwartzman, and Gensler Families.\n\nView full text--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHigh Resolution Direct Detection ... preview & related info | Mendeley (https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/6137f3aa-4c32-338a-98a8-ae55511d52a2/)\nciteturn196search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # High Resolution Direct Detection and Position Determination of Sources with Intermittent Emission ... This paper presents our research findings on the direct detection and position determination (DDPD) problem, which is to estimate the positions and the total number of multiple sources with intermittent emission, using the received signals intercepted by a moving antenna array at multiple observation slots. ... Finally, we find the peaks of the reconstructed MVDR spectrum, which are corresponding to the source positions.\nJournal Article OPEN ACCESS\n\n# High Resolution Direct Detection and Position Determination of Sources with Intermittent Emission\n\n  * Hao K\n  * Wan Q\n  * IEEE Access (2019) 7 43428-43437\n\nDOI: 10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2907136\n\n19 Citations\n\nCitations of this article\n\n5 Readers\n\nMendeley users who have this article in their library.\n\nAdd to library\n\nView PDF\n\nThis article is free to access.\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Keywords (5)\n\n#### Abstract\n\nThis paper presents our research findings on the direct detection and position determination (DDPD) problem, which is to estimate the positions and the total number of multiple sources with intermittent emission, using the received signals intercepted by a moving antenna array at multiple observation slots. We combine the direct position determination (DPD) concept and the Minimum Variance Distortionless Response (MVDR) criterion to solve the DDPD problem without the prior knowledge of the effective number of sources (the number of sources at every observation slot). We find out that the parallel combination of the MVDR spectrum in the cost function results in its own sensitivity to the missing emission, which makes the location estimator bias. Therefore, we use the K-means clustering algorithm to identify the sources with intermittent emission and their emitting slots adaptively, based on the MVDR spectrum values over all observation slots. Then, we use the spectrum values of the identified emitting slots to reconstruct the MVDR spectrum. Finally, we find the peaks of the reconstructed MVDR spectrum, which are corresponding to the source positions. The results of the simulations demonstrate that the proposed method gets asymptotic performance with the signal to noise ratio (SNR), both in the aspects of the root mean square error (RMSE) and the bias error, and high resolution as a generalized MVDR based method.\n\n#### Author supplied keywords\n\n  * Direct position determination\n  * array processing\n  * maximum likelihood\n  * minimum variance distortionless response (MVDR)\n  * passive localization\n\nImage\n\n#### Cite\n\nCITATION STYLE\n\nAPA\n\n[Input]\n\nHao, K., & Wan, Q. (2019). High Resolution Direct Detection and Position Determination of Sources with Intermittent Emission. IEEE Access, 7, 43428–43437. https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2907136--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nclarida28.PDF (https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w8601/w8601.pdf)\nciteturn196search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 25.0 years ago; Davies, R.B.(1977), “Hypothesis Testing when a Nuisance Parameter is Present Only Under the Alternative”, *Biometrika*, 64, 247-254. ... (1987), “Hypothesis Testing when a Nuisance Parameter is Present Only Under the Alternative”, *Biometrika*, 74, 33-43.\nDavies, R.B. (1977), “Hypothesis Testing when a Nuisance Parameter is Present Only Under the Alternative”, *Biometrika*, 64, 247-254.\n\nDavies, R.B. (1987), “Hypothesis Testing when a Nuisance Parameter is Present Only Under the Alter